WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.100
An ABI includes much more than exported function names.

2
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:21.320
It can include calling convention, symbol spelling, name mangling, object size, alignment, padding, field order, vtable layout, exception behavior, RTTI behavior, enum size assumptions, structure packing, and standard library type layout.

3
00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:32.220
It can also include expectations about compiler flags, runtime libraries, allocation functions, thread-local storage, and the way initialization and shutdown occur.

4
00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:37.460
These details matter because compiled code relies on them without rechecking the source.

5
00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:45.320
If a caller believes a structure has one size and the library writes according to another, memory may be interpreted incorrectly.

6
00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:51.500
If two components disagree about calling convention, parameters may not be passed as intended.

7
00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:59.520
If one module allocates with one runtime and another frees with a different runtime, cleanup may be unsafe or unpredictable.

8
00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:04.680
A boundary is safer when these assumptions are visible and documented.

9
00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:08.780
Engineers should not treat ABI as an invisible compiler detail.

10
00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:22.240
Stable binary contracts need explicit decisions about which data types are exposed, which build modes are supported, how errors are reported, how memory is released, and which dependencies form part of the compatibility promise.

11
00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:28.740
The more assumptions are left implicit, the easier it is for a routine change to become a breaking binary change.

12
00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:34.040
Documentation should name both what is guaranteed and what is intentionally unsupported.

13
00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:36.960
A useful ABI inventory is concrete.

14
00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:47.780
It names supported compilers, architectures, build modes, runtime libraries, public symbols, stable data formats, ownership rules, and version negotiation behavior.

15
00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:58.680
That inventory helps reviewers spot when a harmless-looking source change actually alters the binary contract that existing customers, plugins, or service components rely on.
